        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An expansion tank gives heated water somewhere to go. In a closed plumbing system, one with a pressure-reducing valve, backflow preventer or check valve between your house and the main, the water that expands as it heats has nowhere to escape, so pressure climbs every time the heater runs. The tank absorbs that extra volume. You need one if your system is closed, which is increasingly common, and it only works if its air pre-charge is set to match your house water pressure before it goes in.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What an Expansion Tank Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water expands when it is heated. Heating a tank of water from typical incoming temperatures to around 120°F increases its volume by a little over one percent, which on a 50-gallon tank works out to roughly half a gallon or more of extra water every time the heater recovers. That sounds small, but water does not compress. If the extra volume has nowhere to go, pressure throughout the plumbing system rises sharply.
          </p>

          <p>
            In older homes, the extra volume simply pushed back into the municipal water main. That is an open system, and it handled thermal expansion without anyone noticing. Many homes today have devices that prevent water from flowing back into the main, which turns the plumbing into a closed system. In a closed system, the pressure spike every time the heater runs is real, and it can push the pressure well above normal levels.
          </p>

          <p>
            An expansion tank is a small steel tank with an internal rubber diaphragm or bladder. One side of the diaphragm connects to your plumbing; the other side holds a cushion of compressed air. When the water heater expands the water, the extra volume pushes into the tank and compresses the air cushion. When you use hot water and the pressure drops, the air pushes the water back out. The pressure in the system stays much more stable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Without that cushion, repeated pressure spikes stress everything connected to the plumbing. The temperature and pressure relief valve on the water heater may start dripping or discharging, because high pressure is exactly what it is designed to relieve. Faucets, toilet fill valves, washing machine hoses and the water heater tank itself all experience more stress, which can shorten their lives.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The System in Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~1%+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water expansion when heated</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">80 psi</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Code limit before a PRV is required</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">40 psi</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common factory pre-charge</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2 gal</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common tank for 40-50 gal heaters</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="when-required" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. When You Need an Expansion Tank
          </h2>

          <p>
            The short answer is that you need one if your plumbing is a closed system. The International Plumbing Code and the Uniform Plumbing Code, which most US jurisdictions base their local codes on, both require a means of controlling thermal expansion where a backflow preventer, check valve, pressure-reducing valve or similar device stops water from flowing back to the main. Local codes vary, so your jurisdiction may have its own specific requirements.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common devices that create a closed system are pressure-reducing valves, often called PRVs, which are installed where incoming water pressure is high; backflow preventers, which some utilities require at the meter; and check valves, which can be built into some water meters. Many homes have one of these without the owner knowing, which is why plumbers often find that an expansion tank is required when a water heater is replaced.
          </p>

          <p>
            To find out whether your system is closed, look at the main water line where it enters the house, near the main shutoff. A PRV is usually a bell-shaped brass valve with an adjustment screw on top. A backflow preventer may be a larger assembly with test ports. Your water utility can also tell you whether the meter has a check valve or backflow device. If any of these are present, assume you need an expansion tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Even in an open system, an expansion tank does little harm and can still smooth out pressure changes, though it is not required by code. In practice, most plumbers install one with any new water heater where the system is closed, because the inspector is likely to require it. It is one of the most common code upgrades in our <a href="/water-heater-replacement-cost/">water heater replacement cost guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Well systems are a special case. A home on a private well already has a pressure tank as part of the well system, and depending on the setup and local code, that tank may or may not handle thermal expansion from the water heater. Ask a plumber familiar with well systems in your area rather than assuming either way.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Closed system, expansion tank. No exceptions worth taking.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If water cannot flow back to the main, the extra volume from heating has to go somewhere. Without a tank, the relief valve on your heater ends up doing the job it was designed for only as an emergency backup.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Size an Expansion Tank
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expansion tank sizing depends mainly on two numbers: the capacity of your water heater and the static water pressure in your home. Manufacturers such as Amtrol and Watts publish sizing charts that cross these two numbers to give a recommended tank size. Following the chart for the tank you are buying is the most reliable approach, because tank sizes and acceptance volumes differ slightly between brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a general guide, a tank of about two gallons is the size most commonly installed on 40- and 50-gallon residential water heaters at typical household pressures. Larger heaters, such as 75- or 80-gallon tanks, or homes with higher water pressure usually call for the next size up, often around four to four and a half gallons. Those are general patterns, not substitutes for the chart.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure matters more than people expect. The higher the house pressure, the more the air cushion is already compressed before any expansion happens, which means less room for expanding water. A home at 80 psi may need a larger tank than a home at 50 psi with the same water heater. That is why measuring your house pressure is the first step in sizing, not an afterthought.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oversizing an expansion tank is generally harmless, aside from costing more and taking up more space. Undersizing it means the tank fills before the heater finishes recovering, so pressure still spikes. If you are between sizes on a chart, the larger option is usually the safer choice.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Heater capacity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical tank size</th>
