                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">House pressure on the maker's chart</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">75-80 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4-4.5 gallons</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pressure and heater capacity on the chart</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Higher pressure homes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often one size up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Measured static pressure, not a guess</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tankless heaters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies by setup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Manufacturer and local code guidance</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="pre-charge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Setting the Pre-Charge to Match House Pressure
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the step most often skipped, and it is the one that decides whether the tank works. Expansion tanks come from the factory with an air pre-charge, commonly 40 psi. If your house water pressure is 60 psi and the tank is installed at 40 psi, the water immediately compresses the air cushion and fills part of the tank before any heating happens. That leaves less room to absorb expansion, so the tank is partly used up before it starts working.
          </p>

          <p>
            The goal is to set the tank's air pre-charge to match your home's static water pressure. To find that pressure, buy an inexpensive water pressure gauge that threads onto a hose bib or laundry faucet. With no water running in the house, open the faucet and read the gauge. That is your static pressure. If it reads above 80 psi, the plumbing code generally requires a pressure-reducing valve, and you should address that first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adjust the tank before installing it, or with the water side of the tank isolated and drained to zero pressure. The air valve on the tank is a standard Schrader valve, like a bicycle or car tire. Check it with a tire pressure gauge, then add air with a bicycle pump or small compressor, or let some out, until it matches your house pressure. Never adjust it while the tank is connected and full of water under pressure, or the reading will be wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recheck the pre-charge periodically, such as when you do your annual water heater maintenance. Air can slowly leak from the tank over time, and a tank that has lost its air charge works poorly. Checking requires isolating the tank and relieving the water pressure on its side, so it is easiest to do while the water heater is already shut down for a flush. Our <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">how to drain a water heater guide</a> covers that routine.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you ever adjust the PRV and change the house pressure, readjust the expansion tank pre-charge to match. A mismatch either way reduces the tank's effectiveness: too low and the tank fills partly with water before it starts working, too high and it does not begin absorbing expansion until pressure rises above normal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where and How to Install It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expansion tanks are normally installed on the cold water supply line feeding the water heater, between the heater and the device that closes the system, and usually close to the heater. Installing on the cold side keeps the bladder away from the hottest water, which helps it last. The tank connects with a tee fitting on the cold line, typically using a threaded connection on the tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mounting orientation is a common question. Many installers hang the tank vertically below the cold line, though tanks can also be mounted above the line or horizontally depending on the manufacturer's instructions. The key point is support. A tank full of water is heavy, and hanging it from the pipe without support stresses the fitting and the line. Use a strap, bracket or other support as the manufacturer specifies.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DIY or plumber</h3>

          <p>
            Installing an expansion tank involves cutting into the cold water line and making a watertight connection, so the job is similar to other basic plumbing tasks. Confident DIYers can do it with the right fittings, but a plumber can do it quickly, often as part of a water heater replacement. Some jurisdictions require a permit for plumbing changes, so check before you start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before installing, shut off the water supply to the heater and relieve pressure by opening a faucet. Set the pre-charge, install the tee and tank with suitable thread sealant, add support, then restore the water supply and check for leaks. If you are replacing the water heater at the same time, this is the natural moment to fit the tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to the water heater's relief valve while you are there. The expansion tank does not replace the temperature and pressure relief valve, which remains a required safety device. It just keeps the system pressure from rising to the point where the relief valve has to open. Our <a href="/water-heater-pressure-relief-valve/">pressure relief valve guide</a> explains how the two work together.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="failure-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Signs Your Expansion Tank Has Failed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expansion tanks do not last forever. The diaphragm or bladder can rupture, and the air charge can leak away. Many tanks last somewhere in the range of five to ten years, though that varies with water quality, pressure and installation. A failed tank behaves like no tank at all, so the symptoms of failure are the same as the symptoms of thermal expansion in a closed system.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common sign is the water heater's relief valve dripping or discharging, especially after the heater has been running. Other signs include pressure gauge readings that climb well above normal after hot water use, banging pipes, and toilets or faucets that run or leak. If you have a gauge on a hose bib, watch it while the heater recovers. A large rise indicates the tank is not absorbing expansion.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can check the tank itself in two simple ways. First, tap it. A working tank should sound hollow on the air side and solid on the water side. A tank that sounds solid all over is likely waterlogged. Second, briefly press the Schrader valve. Air should come out. If water comes out instead, the diaphragm has failed and water has filled the air side.
          </p>

          <p>
            A failed expansion tank is replaced, not repaired. Replacing it involves the same steps as a new installation: shut off the water, relieve pressure, remove the old tank, set the pre-charge on the new one, install and check for leaks. Some installers fit a ball valve between the tee and tank, which makes future replacement and pre-charge checks easier.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your expansion tank has failed and your water heater is also getting old, it may make sense to replace both at once. Our <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">guide to how long water heaters last</a> helps you judge where your heater is in its life, and our <a href="/water-heater-anode-rod-replacement/">anode rod replacement guide</a> covers the other maintenance job that most affects tank life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Alternatives, Cost and What Is Worth Doing
          </h2>

          <p>
            An expansion tank is the most common way to control thermal expansion, but it is not the only one. A thermal expansion relief valve, a small valve installed on the cold line that opens to discharge a little water when pressure rises, is another option some codes accept. It is cheaper to install but wastes water every time it opens and needs a place to drain. For most homes, a properly sized and charged expansion tank is the better long-term solution.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expansion tanks themselves are inexpensive at retail, and a two-gallon residential tank is one of the cheaper parts in a plumbing system. The cost of installation depends on whether it is done as part of a water heater replacement, where it adds little, or as a separate job, where a plumber's service call makes up most of the bill. Check with local plumbers for current pricing in your area.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your system is closed, an expansion tank is worth it. The cost is small compared with the potential damage from repeated pressure spikes, and it is often required by code. If your system is open, there is usually no need for one, though there is little harm in having one. In either case, the most valuable related step is measuring your house pressure, because pressure above 80 psi is a problem on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are buying a new water heater, ask the installer whether your system is closed and whether the quote includes an expansion tank. Our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heater guide</a> covers tank models, and our <a href="/best-electric-water-heater/">best electric water heater guide</a> covers electric options, both of which benefit from proper expansion control.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, treat the expansion tank as part of your annual maintenance routine. Check the air pre-charge, tap the tank, and look for leaks at the connection. A few minutes a year keeps it working and keeps your water heater, relief valve and fixtures from taking the strain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing the tank at its factory pre-charge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most tanks ship at about 40 psi. If your house pressure is higher, water fills part of the tank before any heating happens and it cannot absorb enough expansion. Measure your static pressure with a gauge and set the tank's air charge to match before you install it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming you have an open system</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many homes have a pressure-reducing valve, backflow preventer or check-valve meter that the owner does not know about. Check the main line where it enters the house, or ask your water utility. If any of these devices is present, the system is closed and needs expansion control.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hanging a full tank from the pipe unsupported</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tank full of water is heavy, and hanging it from a threaded fitting stresses the joint and the line. Follow the manufacturer's mounting instructions and add a strap or bracket. Proper support prevents leaks and keeps the connection from loosening over time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a dripping relief valve</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dripping temperature and pressure relief valve is often a sign of thermal expansion or high house pressure, not a bad valve. Replacing the valve without fixing the cause does not solve the problem. Check for a closed system, test the expansion tank and measure your water pressure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never checking the tank after installation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Expansion tanks lose air over time and their bladders can fail. A waterlogged tank works no better than no tank. Tap it and check the air valve once a year during water heater maintenance, and replace it if water comes out of the valve or it sounds solid all over.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an expansion tank for my water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need one if your plumbing is a closed system, meaning a pressure-reducing valve, backflow preventer or check valve stops water flowing back to the main. Most US plumbing codes require thermal expansion control in that case. If your system is open, an expansion tank is generally not required, though local code may differ.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size expansion tank do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your water heater capacity and your house water pressure. A tank of about two gallons is common for 40- to 50-gallon heaters at typical pressure, while larger heaters or higher pressure often need a bigger tank. Use the manufacturer's sizing chart for the tank you buy, and choose the larger option if you fall between sizes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should the pressure be in an expansion tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Set the tank's air pre-charge to match your home's static water pressure, measured with a gauge on a hose bib with no water running. Tanks commonly ship at about 40 psi, so you will often need to add air with a bicycle pump. Adjust it before installation or with the water side isolated and drained.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should an expansion tank be installed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually on the cold water supply line feeding the water heater, between the heater and the device that closes the system, and close to the heater. The cold side keeps the bladder away from the hottest water. Support the tank with a strap or bracket according to the manufacturer's instructions rather than hanging it from the pipe alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my expansion tank is bad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Common signs include a dripping relief valve on the water heater, rising pressure after hot water use, and banging pipes. Tap the tank: it should sound hollow on top and solid at the bottom. If it sounds solid all over or water comes out when you press the air valve, the bladder has failed and the tank needs replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a water heater expansion tank last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many expansion tanks last around five to ten years, depending on water quality, house pressure and installation. Checking the air charge every year helps catch problems early. When the bladder fails, the tank must be replaced rather than repaired, and it often makes sense to replace it along with an aging water heater.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a tankless water heater need an expansion tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. Tankless heaters hold far less water than a tank, so thermal expansion is much smaller, but some manufacturers and local codes still call for expansion control in closed systems. Check the installation manual for your heater and your local code, or ask your installer, rather than assuming either way.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

