        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If your plumbing is closed, an expansion tank is not optional. It absorbs the water that expands every time your heater runs, keeps pressure stable, and spares your relief valve, fixtures and tank from repeated spikes. Size it with the manufacturer's chart and set its pre-charge to match your measured house pressure before it goes in.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Check it once a year when you service the water heater, and replace it when the bladder fails. If your heater is due for replacement too, our gas water heater picks are the place to start, and the expansion tank is one line on that quote worth keeping.
          </p>
        </div>

